-- title:
--   Descent of approximate G-invariants under flat base change
-- statement:
--   Let $\mathcal{O}$ be a commutative domain which is a discrete valuation ring, let $\pi \in \mathcal{O}$ be irreducible, and assume $\mathcal{O}$ is adically complete for the ideal $(\pi)$. Let $R$ be a commutative $\mathcal{O}$-algebra which is adically complete for the ideal generated by the image of $\pi$, on which that image is a non-zero-divisor (if $\pi x = 0$ then $x = 0$), and such that $R/\pi R$ is of finite type over $\mathcal{O}$; let $G$ be a finite group acting on $R$ by ring automorphisms, the action commuting with the $\mathcal{O}$-scalar multiplication, and write $A = R^{G}$ for the $\mathcal{O}$-subalgebra of fixed points. The assertion is the existence of an exponent $e \in \mathbb{N}$, depending only on these data, such that the following holds for every commutative ring $A'$ that is an $A$-algebra and is flat as an $A$-module: for every family $\rho$ of additive endomorphisms of $R \otimes_{A} A'$ indexed by $G$ satisfying $\rho(g)(r \otimes s) = (g \cdot r) \otimes s$, every $m \in \mathbb{N}$ and every $x \in R \otimes_{A} A'$ with $\rho(g)x - x$ in the ideal of $R \otimes_{A} A'$ generated by $\pi^{m+e} \otimes 1$ for all $g \in G$, there is $w \in A'$ with $x - 1 \otimes w$ in the ideal generated by $\pi^{m} \otimes 1$.
--
--   This is the statement that formation of $G$-invariants commutes with flat base change up to a bounded shift in the $\pi$-adic filtration: an element of $R \otimes_A A'$ invariant modulo $\pi^{m+e}$ is congruent modulo $\pi^{m}$ to an element of $A'$, with the loss $e$ uniform in $A'$, $m$ and $x$. It is used in the construction of quotients of admissible formal algebras by finite group actions, feeding into [`AlgebraicGeometry.AdmissibleAlgebra.isAdicComplete_fixedPoints_and_exists_ringEquiv_quotient_of_ringEquiv_tensorProduct_quotient`](thm.html#AlgebraicGeometry.AdmissibleAlgebra.isAdicComplete_fixedPoints_and_exists_ringEquiv_quotient_of_ringEquiv_tensorProduct_quotient); the uniform exponent comes from bounded $\pi$-power torsion in the first cohomology of $G$ acting on $R$, which is finite over the Noetherian ring $A$.
